Kidtimer now controls only running the blacklisted executables (instead of
controlling the login session). The blacklisted executables are listed in

   $dbdir/executables.blacklist (/var/local/kidtimer/executables.blacklist).

Only one exact real full path is required (no symbolic links) per line. See the
provided sample file for blacklisting Firefox.

ATTENTION: Since no user login control will occur after upgrading to this
version of kidtimer, please make sure that the kids users are unlocked
(passwd -u <username>) so they can log in.

Now the DB files are moved to /var/local/kidtimer and are directly editable.
This directory can also be mounted from a fileserver for shared use.

Internet site visits can also be restricted with the help of libnss-hostfilter.
This mechanism filters host name resolution (DNS) queries and when time is up
for a user, blacklisted hosts are prevented from being resolved for him/her. The
blacklisted hosts are listed in

   $dbdir/hosts.blacklist (/var/local/kidtimer/hosts.blacklist).

One hostname pattern is allowed per line. Pattern mathing is done as of the Bash
manual (with extglob option enabled). See the provided sample file for
blacklisting Youtube and Facebook. Host name resolution attempts of kidtimer
users are logged to syslog, so the blacklist can be adjusted by analyzing user
behaviour.
Setting up blacklisted host handling requires a bit of preparation, so please
look into the installation instructions below.
The steps #2, #3, #5 and adjusting the hosts blacklist is also necessary when
upgrading to kidtimer v2.18.

### NOTE from the original author:
I have been looking for a solution to this for a long time. I have two kids and wish to restrict their time on the computers. Since nothing else seems to work out there, I decided to write my own. I call it kidtimer. Very original, I know. :)

Kidtimer should work on any recent version of Ubuntu, both 32 & 64 bit. Should also work on Debian too. It uses standard Linux utilities and notify-send (for user notification).

This is how to install it and use it;

1. Download the latest kidtimer deb file;
https://github.com/psagi/kidtimer/raw/master/DEBS/kidtimer_latest.deb

2. If you would like to use the Internet site blacklisting feature, download
libnss-hostfilter for your architecture, too.
https://github.com/psagi/libnss-hostfilter/raw/master/release/
(For architectures there is no debian package built, you can always build it
from source.) 

3. Install libnss-hostfilter (if Internet host blacklisting is required):
<pre>
sudo dpkg -i libnss_hostfilter*.deb
sudo apt-get -f install
</pre>
Configure it according to its documentation.

4. Install it using Software Center or use the following lines;
<pre>
sudo dpkg -i kidtimer*.deb
sudo apt-get -f install
</pre>

5. If Internet host blacklisting is reguired, configure the hostfilter helper command of libnss-hostfilter according to its documentation to be the one provided by kidtimer. See the example configuration file

/usr/share/kidtimer/doc/examples/hostfilter.conf.

6. Run kidtimer:
<pre>
sudo kidtimer
</pre>

7. Setup a existing user account.

8. Done.

For help:
<pre>
sudo kidtimer help
</pre>

Add time to a user's account (for that day only):
<pre>
sudo kidtimer addtime user minutes
</pre>

To check user's time:
<pre>
sudo kidtimer gettime user
</pre>


Features:

Allow your kid specific hours of the day to run blacklisted executables and
access blacklisted Internet sites, both weekday and weekend.

Set maximum amount of time, both weekday and weekend.

Keeps track of reward balance that is persistent across days.

Time control can be temporarily suspended/resumed within the day.


Cronjobs:

Check every minute to see if user is logged in. If so, increment total time. If
hit max time, revoke exec permission from blacklisted executables and prevent
accessing blacklisted Internet sites and start logoff sequence (5 mins total)
that will result in killing those.

Check hourly to see if user is allowed to use the blacklisted programs, sites.

At midnight, reset time.

The application uses notify-send to alert user time is running out. When time runs out, blacklisted user processes are terminated, so please prepare user.

Enjoy!
